Two functions test whether a list of $n$ numbers contains a repeated value. Both return the same results (`True True` for `[3, 8, 5, 8]`, `False False` for `[1, 2, 3]`).

```python
def has_dup_a(lst):
    for i in range(len(lst)):
        for j in range(i + 1, len(lst)):
            if lst[i] == lst[j]:
                return True
    return False

def has_dup_b(lst):
    seen = set()
    for x in lst:
        if x in seen:
            return True
        seen.add(x)
    return False
```

(a) State the worst-case time complexity of each function. (2 marks)

(b) State the extra memory each one needs, and say which function you would choose for a list of 100,000 items and why. (4 marks)
